      blocks: [
        {
          type: 'text',
          heading: '1. The Web Was Born Dead',
          paragraphs: [
            "In the early 1990s, a web page was a static document. It could show text and images, and that was essentially all.",
            "There was no way for a page to react to you. If you filled in a form and made a mistake, the page could not tell you on the spot. It had to ship your data all the way to a distant server and wait for the server to send back a reply just to say \"that's wrong, try again.\""
          ]
        },
        {
          type: 'takeaway',
          title: 'Key Takeaway',
          text: 'Netscape realized the web needed to come alive. It needed to respond instantly, right there in the page.'
        },
        {
          type: 'text',
          heading: '2. Enter Sun, and a Language Called Java',
          paragraphs: [
            "A company called Sun Microsystems had created a programming language named Java.",
            "Java's superpower was a promise:"
          ]
        },
        {
          type: 'quote',
          text: '“Write once, run anywhere.”'
        },
        {
          type: 'text',
          paragraphs: [
            "A Java program didn't care whether you were on Windows, Mac, or Linux — it would run the same everywhere, inside a piece of software called the Java Virtual Machine."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    summary: 'JavaScript was created in May 1995 by Brendan Eich at Netscape. Designed under tight deadlines as a "glue language" to make HTML pages interactive, it merged concepts from Scheme, Self, and Java. It is now standardized under ECMAScript and runs on billions of devices.',
