  {
    id: 'how-works',
    title: 'How JS Works',
    heading: 'How JS Works Under the Hood',
    subtitle: 'An introduction to compiler threads, call stacks, memory heaps, and the event loop.',
    badges: [
      { icon: Clock, text: '18 min read', color: 'text-purple-400' },
      { icon: Sparkles, text: 'Under the Hood', color: 'text-[#C084FC]' },
      { icon: Compass, text: 'Intermediate', color: 'text-space-yellow' },
      { icon: Layers, text: 'Engine Architecture', color: 'text-blue-400' }
    ],
    notes: {
      blocks: [
        {
          type: 'text',
          heading: '1. Engine Execution & Call Stack',
          paragraphs: [
            "The JavaScript engine (such as Google's V8) consists of two main components: the Memory Heap and the Call Stack.",
            "The Memory Heap is where memory allocation happens (storing large object references), while the Call Stack is where execution contexts are created. JavaScript is single-threaded, meaning it has only one Call Stack and executes one instruction at a time."
          ]
        },
        {
          type: 'takeaway',
          title: 'Key Takeaway',
          text: 'Because JS is single-threaded, any function running on the Call Stack blocks other computations. To execute long tasks without freezing the page, the engine delegates asynchronous operations to browser Web APIs.'
        },
        {
          type: 'text',
          heading: '2. The Event Loop Cycle',
          paragraphs: [
            "When asynchronous code (like a timer or network call) completes, its callback is sent to the Task Queue (or Callback Queue).",
            "The Event Loop is a continuous loop whose sole job is to monitor the Call Stack. If the Call Stack is empty, it pulls the first task from the queue and pushes it onto the stack for execution."
          ]
        },
        {
          type: 'quote',
          text: '“Stack pushes represent synchronous execution, while queues represent queued events.”'
        }
      ]
    },
    summary: 'The JS engine executes code on a single thread using a LIFO Call Stack. It allocates memory for objects in the Memory Heap. Asynchronous callbacks are scheduled through browser Web APIs and pushed back onto the stack via the Event Loop.',
    codeHtml: `<span class="code-comment">// Tracing call execution contexts</span>
<span class="code-keyword">function</span> <span class="code-function">multiply</span>(<span class="code-variable">a</span>, <span class="code-variable">b</span>) { <span class="code-keyword">return</span> <span class="code-variable">a</span> <span class="code-operator">*</span> <span class="code-variable">b</span>; }
<span class="code-keyword">function</span> <span class="code-function">square</span>(<span class="code-variable">n</span>) { <span class="code-keyword">return</span> <span class="code-function">multiply</span>(<span class="code-variable">n</span>, <span class="code-variable">n</span>); }

<span class="code-variable">console</span>.<span class="code-function">log</span>(<span class="code-function">square</span>(<span class="code-number">4</span>));`,
    sandboxCode: `// Simulating execution stack trace
function traceTaskOne() {
  console.log(" -> [1] Task One executing...");
  return "Finished One";
}

function runEngine() {
  console.log("[0] Main script thread started...");
  const res = traceTaskOne();
  console.log("[2] Main script thread ended. Result:", res);
}

runEngine();`,
    quiz: {
      question: 'What runtime engine component is responsible for transferring callbacks from the task queue to the call stack when the stack is empty?',
      options: [
        'Memory Heap manager',
        'The Event Loop',
        'Syntax Parser compiler',
        'Garbage Collector thread'
      ],
      answerIdx: 1,
      explanation: 'The Event Loop checks the Call Stack. When it is empty, it feeds callback operations from the Task Queue/Microtask Queue into the stack.'
    },
    references: [
      { name: 'MDN Web Docs: Concurrency Model and the Event Loop', url: 'https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Event_loop' }
    ]
  },
