  {
    id: 'data-primitives',
    title: 'Primitives vs Objects',
    heading: 'Memory Management: Primitives vs Objects',
    subtitle: 'Understanding Stack and Heap memory allocations and why it governs value copy versus reference assignment.',
    badges: [
      { icon: Clock, text: '12 min read', color: 'text-purple-400' },
      { icon: Sparkles, text: 'Memory Architecture', color: 'text-[#C084FC]' },
      { icon: Compass, text: 'Beginner', color: 'text-space-yellow' },
      { icon: Layers, text: 'First Principles', color: 'text-blue-400' }
    ],
    notes: {
      blocks: [
        {
          type: 'text',
          heading: '1. Stack Memory and Primitives',
          paragraphs: [
            "JavaScript has 7 primitive types: Number, String, Boolean, null, undefined, Symbol, and BigInt.",
            "Primitives are stored directly on the Stack. The stack is a fast, structured memory space of fixed size. When you declare a primitive, JavaScript allocates a small chunk of stack memory to hold its literal value.",
            "Primitive values are immutable. You cannot mutate the value 'hello' to become 'Hello' in place; any operation that modifies a primitive returns a brand-new value in a separate memory slot."
          ]
        },
        {
          type: 'takeaway',
          title: 'Key Takeaway',
          text: 'Because primitives are stored directly in stack slots, assigning one variable to another copies the actual value. They are completely independent.'
        },
        {
          type: 'text',
          heading: '2. Heap Memory and Objects',
          paragraphs: [
            "Objects (including Arrays and Functions) are of dynamic size. They cannot be stored in fixed stack slots. Instead, they are allocated inside the Memory Heap, a large, unstructured pool of memory.",
            "When you create an object, the engine allocates heap space for its properties. It then stores a pointer (the memory address of the heap location) in a stack slot associated with your variable.",
            "When you assign one object variable to another, you copy the pointer, not the object itself. Both variables now point to the exact same heap reference."
          ]
        },
        {
          type: 'quote',
          text: '“Variables hold values for primitives, but they hold addresses for objects.”'
        }
      ]
    },
    summary: 'Primitives (Number, String, Boolean, null, undefined, Symbol, BigInt) are immutable values stored directly on the Stack and copied by value. Objects (including Arrays and Functions) are mutable structures stored in the Heap and copied by reference (pointer address).',
    codeHtml: `<span class="code-comment">// Primitives copy by value</span>
<span class="code-keyword">let</span> <span class="code-variable">x</span> <span class="code-operator">=</span> <span class="code-number">10</span>;
<span class="code-keyword">let</span> <span class="code-variable">y</span> <span class="code-operator">=</span> <span class="code-variable">x</span>;
<span class="code-variable">y</span> <span class="code-operator">=</span> <span class="code-number">20</span>; <span class="code-comment">// x remains 10</span>
<br/>
<span class="code-comment">// Objects copy by reference</span>
<span class="code-keyword">const</span> <span class="code-variable">obj1</span> <span class="code-operator">=</span> { <span class="code-variable">value</span>: <span class="code-number">10</span> };
<span class="code-keyword">const</span> <span class="code-variable">obj2</span> <span class="code-operator">=</span> <span class="code-variable">obj1</span>;
<span class="code-variable">obj2</span>.<span class="code-variable">value</span> <span class="code-operator">=</span> <span class="code-number">20</span>; <span class="code-comment">// obj1.value is now 20</span>`,
    sandboxCode: `// Modify these variables to see stack vs heap in action
let a = "initial";
let b = a;
b = "changed";

console.log("Primitive Copy Check:");
console.log("a:", a); // "initial"
console.log("b:", b); // "changed"

console.log("\nObject Reference Check:");
const spacecraft = { name: "Voyager 1" };
const clone = spacecraft;
clone.name = "Voyager 2";

console.log("spacecraft.name:", spacecraft.name); // "Voyager 2"
console.log("clone.name:", clone.name); // "Voyager 2"`,
    quiz: {
      question: 'What happens in memory when you assign `let obj2 = obj1` where `obj1` is an object?',
      options: [
        'A full clone of the object is created in the heap.',
        'The variable obj2 is assigned a copy of the pointer address pointing to the same heap location.',
        'The object is moved from the stack to the heap.',
        'An error is thrown because objects are immutable.'
      ],
      answerIdx: 1,
      explanation: 'Assigning one object variable to another copies the memory address pointer. Both variables reference the exact same object in the heap.'
    },
    references: [
      { name: 'MDN: JavaScript data types and data structures', url: 'https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Data_structures' }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: 'data-coercion',
    title: 'Type Coercion & Truthiness',
    heading: 'Type Coercion & Truthiness Heuristics',
    subtitle: 'Demystifying implicit type conversions and how JavaScript evaluates conditions.',
    badges: [
      { icon: Clock, text: '12 min read', color: 'text-purple-400' },
      { icon: Sparkles, text: 'Coercion', color: 'text-[#C084FC]' },
      { icon: Compass, text: 'Intermediate', color: 'text-space-yellow' },
      { icon: Layers, text: 'Language Quirks', color: 'text-blue-400' }
    ],
    notes: {
      blocks: [
        {
          type: 'text',
          heading: '1. Implicit vs Explicit Coercion',
          paragraphs: [
            "JavaScript is a dynamically and weakly typed language. When performing operations with mismatched types, the engine performs implicit coercion rather than throwing a type error.",
            "Explicit coercion is when you deliberately convert types (e.g. `Number('42')`). Implicit coercion happens behind the scenes (e.g. `'42' - 2` becomes `40` because the mathematical minus operator casts `'42'` to a number)."
          ]
        },
        {
          type: 'takeaway',
          title: 'Key Takeaway',
          text: 'The subtraction (-) and multiplication (*) operators only exist for mathematical operations, forcing number coercion. However, the addition (+) operator is overloaded for string concatenation, forcing string coercion if either side is a string.'
        },
        {
          type: 'text',
          heading: '2. Truthy vs Falsy Evaluation',
          paragraphs: [
            "Every value in JavaScript has an inherent boolean state. When evaluated in a conditional (like an `if` statement), values are coerced to true or false.",
            "There are exactly 8 falsy values in JS: false, 0, -0, 0n (BigInt zero), \"\" (empty string), null, undefined, and NaN. Everything else is truthy (including empty arrays `[]` and empty objects `{}`)."
          ]
        },
        {
          type: 'quote',
          text: '“Coercion is the price we pay for dynamic expression evaluation.”'
        }
      ]
    },
    summary: 'Type coercion is the implicit or explicit conversion of values from one data type to another. Boolean evaluation classifies values as falsy (exactly eight states including null and 0) or truthy (everything else, including empty arrays).',
    codeHtml: `<span class="code-comment">// Concatenation vs Arithmetic</span>
<span class="code-variable">console</span>.<span class="code-function">log</span>(<span class="code-string">"1"</span> <span class="code-operator">+</span> <span class="code-number">1</span>); <span class="code-comment">// "11" (String)</span>
<span class="code-variable">console</span>.<span class="code-function">log</span>(<span class="code-string">"10"</span> <span class="code-operator">-</span> <span class="code-number">2</span>); <span class="code-comment">// 8 (Number)</span>
<br/>
<span class="code-comment">// Truthiness check</span>
<span class="code-keyword">if</span> ([]) {
  <span class="code-variable">console</span>.<span class="code-function">log</span>(<span class="code-string">"Empty arrays are truthy!"</span>);
}`,
    sandboxCode: `// Experiment with implicit coercion results
console.log("Coercion Heuristics:");
console.log("'5' + 5 =", "5" + 5);
console.log("'5' - 5 =", "5" - 5);
console.log("false == 0 =", false == 0);

console.log("\nTruthy checks:");
console.log("Boolean([]):", Boolean([])); // true
console.log("Boolean(null):", Boolean(null)); // false`,
    quiz: {
      question: 'Which of the following values is evaluated as TRUTHY in a conditional expression?',
      options: [
        '"" (empty string)',
        '[] (empty array)',
        '0 (number zero)',
        'undefined'
      ],
      answerIdx: 1,
      explanation: 'Empty arrays `[]` and empty objects `{}` are objects, and all objects are truthy in JavaScript, regardless of their contents.'
    },
    references: [
      { name: 'MDN Web Docs: Type Coercion', url: 'https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/Type_coercion' }
    ]
  },
  {
    id: 'ops-arithmetic',
    title: 'Arithmetic & Type Casting',
    heading: 'Arithmetic Operators and Casting',
    subtitle: 'First principles of operation precedence, overloading, and string conversions.',
    badges: [
      { icon: Clock, text: '10 min read', color: 'text-purple-400' },
      { icon: Sparkles, text: 'Arithmetic', color: 'text-[#C084FC]' },
      { icon: Compass, text: 'Beginner', color: 'text-space-yellow' },
      { icon: Layers, text: 'Evaluation', color: 'text-blue-400' }
    ],
    notes: {
      blocks: [
        {
          type: 'text',
          heading: '1. The Concatenation Override',
          paragraphs: [
            "In JavaScript, operators are processed using the specifications defined in ECMA-262. The addition operator (+) is unique because it handles both numerical addition and string concatenation.",
            "If either operand is a string, JavaScript converts the other operand to a string and concatenates them. If neither is a string, both are coerced to numbers (via the `ToNumber` abstract operation) and added mathematically."
          ]
        },
        {
          type: 'takeaway',
          title: 'Key Takeaway',
          text: 'Non-addition arithmetic operators (-, *, /, %) have no string-based behavior. If you pass a string to them, they will always attempt to coerce it to a number. If it fails, they return NaN (Not a Number).'
        },
        {
          type: 'text',
          heading: '2. Operator Precedence and Evaluation Order',
          paragraphs: [
            "Operator precedence determines the order in which operators are evaluated. Multiplicative operators (*, /, %) have higher precedence than additive operators (+, -).",
            "Operators of the same precedence are evaluated from left to right (left-associative). Parentheses can be used to explicitly override precedence."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    summary: 'The plus (+) operator is overloaded for string concatenation and numerical addition. Other arithmetic operators always cast values to numbers or evaluate to NaN. Precedence controls evaluation sequences.',
    codeHtml: `<span class="code-comment">// Precedence and coercion</span>
<span class="code-keyword">const</span> <span class="code-variable">result</span> <span class="code-operator">=</span> <span class="code-number">10</span> <span class="code-operator">+</span> <span class="code-number">5</span> <span class="code-operator">*</span> <span class="code-string">"2"</span>; <span class="code-comment">// 5 * "2" is 10, plus 10 is 20</span>
<span class="code-variable">console</span>.<span class="code-function">log</span>(<span class="code-variable">result</span>);
<br/>
<span class="code-comment">// division coercion</span>
<span class="code-variable">console</span>.<span class="code-function">log</span>(<span class="code-string">"20"</span> <span class="code-operator">/</span> <span class="code-number">2</span>); <span class="code-comment">// 10</span>`,
    sandboxCode: `// Telemetry tests for mathematics
console.log("Evaluation details:");
console.log("10 - '2' * 3 =", 10 - '2' * 3); // 4
console.log("'10' - '2' =", '10' - '2'); // 8
console.log("10 + 5 + 'px' =", 10 + 5 + 'px'); // '15px'`,
